// Course content. To add a lesson, copy one object and change it.
// steps: [level, html]  (1 = foundation, 2 = core, 3 = going deeper). Maths uses $...$ (KaTeX).
// qs: {q, o: options, a: index of correct option, e: explanation}
const COURSE = [
{ id: "fn", t: "1. Functions and transformations", steps: [
  [1, "A <b>function</b> is a machine: one number in, one number out. $f(x)=2x+1$ means 'double it, add 1', so $f(3)=7$. Each input gives exactly one output."],
  [2, "Change the machine and the graph moves. For $y=a\\,f(b(x-h))+k$: <b>h</b> slides it right, <b>k</b> slides it up, <b>a</b> stretches it vertically (negative flips it), <b>b</b> squashes it sideways by $1/b$. So $y=(x-2)^2+1$ is $y=x^2$ moved right 2 and up 1: vertex $(2,1)$."],
  [2, "<b>Composite</b> $f(g(x))$: do the inside first (socks, then shoes). With $f(x)=x^2$ and $g(x)=x+3$: $f(g(2))=f(5)=25$ but $g(f(2))=g(4)=7$. Order matters."],
  [3, "<b>Inverse</b>: undo the machine. Write $y=f(x)$, swap $x$ and $y$, solve for $y$. For $f(x)=3x-4$: $x=3y-4$ so $f^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+4}{3}$. Its graph is the original mirrored in $y=x$. It only exists if $f$ is one-to-one, which is why $x^2$ is restricted to $x\\ge0$ before taking $\\sqrt{x}$."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "What is the vertex of $y=(x-2)^2+1$?", o: ["(2, 1)", "(-2, 1)", "(2, -1)", "(1, 2)"], a: 0, e: "'x - 2' moves the graph RIGHT 2 and '+1' moves it up 1." },
  { q: "If $f(x)=x^2$ and $g(x)=x+3$, what is $f(g(2))$?", o: ["7", "25", "13", "10"], a: 1, e: "Inside first: g(2)=5, then f(5)=25." },
  { q: "The inverse of $f(x)=3x-4$ is:", o: ["(x-4)/3", "(x+4)/3", "3x+4", "1/(3x-4)"], a: 1, e: "Swap: x=3y-4, so y=(x+4)/3." }] },

{ id: "chain", t: "2. Derivatives and the chain rule", steps: [
  [1, "The <b>derivative</b> is the slope of the curve at one exact point, i.e. a <b>rate of change</b>. Draw a line through two points on the curve and slide one toward the other: the line becomes the tangent, and its slope is the derivative."],
  [2, "Core rules: $(x^n)'=nx^{n-1}$, $(e^x)'=e^x$, $(\\sin x)'=\\cos x$, $(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x$, $(\\ln x)'=\\frac1x$. Example: slope of $y=x^3$ at $x=2$ is $3\\cdot2^2=12$."],
  [2, "<b>Chain rule</b> (a function inside a function): differentiate the outside, leave the inside alone, then multiply by the derivative of the inside. $\\frac{d}{dx}(3x+1)^5=5(3x+1)^4\\cdot3$. And $\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin(3x)=3\\cos(3x)$. Forgetting the 'times inside derivative' is the most common mistake."],
  [3, "<b>From first principles</b>: for $f(x)=x^2$ the slope between $x$ and $x+h$ is $\\frac{(x+h)^2-x^2}{h}=2x+h$. Let $h\\to0$ and you get $2x$. Every rule is proved by this limit idea."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "$\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin(3x)$ equals:", o: ["cos 3x", "3cos 3x", "-3cos 3x", "3sin 3x"], a: 1, e: "Outside: sin becomes cos. Inside derivative: 3." },
  { q: "The slope of $y=x^3$ at $x=2$ is:", o: ["6", "8", "12", "3"], a: 2, e: "y' = 3x², so 3 x 4 = 12." },
  { q: "$\\frac{d}{dx}e^{2x}$ equals:", o: ["e^{2x}", "2e^{2x}", "2x e^{2x-1}", "e^2"], a: 1, e: "Chain rule: e^{2x} stays, times the inside derivative 2." }] },

{ id: "int", t: "3. Integration as area", steps: [
  [1, "Chop the area under a curve into thin rectangles and add them. Make them thinner and thinner: the total is the <b>integral</b> $\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx$. The ∫ sign is a stretched 'S' for sum."],
  [2, "Integration also <b>undoes</b> differentiation. $\\int x^n dx=\\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}+c$. <b>Fundamental Theorem</b>: $\\int_a^b f\\,dx=F(b)-F(a)$ where $F$ is an antiderivative. Example: $\\int_0^3 2x\\,dx=[x^2]_0^3=9$."],
  [2, "If the inside is linear, divide by its slope: $\\int e^{2x}dx=\\frac12e^{2x}+c$ and $\\int\\cos3x\\,dx=\\frac13\\sin3x+c$. <b>Area between curves</b> = $\\int(\\text{top}-\\text{bottom})\\,dx$."],
  [3, "<b>Why the two jobs match</b>: let $A(x)$ be the area up to $x$. Adding a sliver of width $h$ adds about $f(x)h$, so $A'(x)=f(x)$. Area-so-far has $f$ as its derivative. That is the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "$\\int_1^4(3x^2-2x)\\,dx=$", o: ["48", "45", "60", "36"], a: 0, e: "Antiderivative x³ - x². At 4: 48. At 1: 0. Answer 48." },
  { q: "$\\int e^{2x}dx=$", o: ["2e^{2x}+c", "½e^{2x}+c", "e^{2x}+c", "e^{x²}+c"], a: 1, e: "Divide by the inside slope 2." },
  { q: "Area between $y=x$ and $y=x^2$ for $0\\le x\\le1$:", o: ["1/2", "1/3", "1/6", "1/12"], a: 2, e: "∫(x - x²)dx = 1/2 - 1/3 = 1/6." }] },

{ id: "cx", t: "4. Complex numbers", steps: [
  [1, "No real number squares to $-1$, so we invent $i$ with $i^2=-1$. A complex number $a+bi$ is just a <b>point on a flat map</b>: $a$ steps across, $b$ steps up. Its size is $|z|=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$; for $3+4i$ that is 5."],
  [2, "Do arithmetic like ordinary brackets, then replace $i^2$ with $-1$: $(2+3i)(1-i)=2-2i+3i-3i^2=5+i$. To divide, multiply top and bottom by the conjugate of the bottom."],
  [2, "<b>Polar form</b>: $z=r(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)$. Multiplying <b>multiplies sizes and adds angles</b>, so multiplication is a rotation plus a stretch. <b>De Moivre</b>: $(r\\,\\mathrm{cis}\\,\\theta)^n=r^n\\,\\mathrm{cis}\\,n\\theta$. $1+i=\\sqrt2\\,\\mathrm{cis}\\frac\\pi4$, so $(1+i)^8=16\\,\\mathrm{cis}\\,2\\pi=16$."],
  [3, "<b>Euler's formula</b> $e^{i\\theta}=\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta$ says raising $e$ to an imaginary power rotates around the unit circle. At $\\theta=\\pi$: $e^{i\\pi}+1=0$. Engineers use this for AC circuits (phasors), signals and control."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "$(2+3i)(1-i)=$", o: ["5 + i", "5 - i", "-1 + i", "2 - 3i"], a: 0, e: "2 - 2i + 3i - 3i² = 2 + i + 3 = 5 + i." },
  { q: "$|3+4i|=$", o: ["7", "5", "25", "√7"], a: 1, e: "√(9+16) = 5." },
  { q: "$(1+i)^8=$", o: ["8", "16", "16i", "-16"], a: 1, e: "(√2)^8 cis(8·π/4) = 16 cis 2π = 16." }] },

{ id: "vec", t: "5. Vectors and the dot product", steps: [
  [1, "A <b>vector</b> has size <i>and</i> direction (a force, a velocity). Write it as components, e.g. $(1,2,2)$. Add tip to tail. Its size is $|a|=\\sqrt{a_1^2+a_2^2+a_3^2}$, so $|(1,2,2)|=3$."],
  [2, "<b>Dot product</b>: $a\\cdot b=a_1b_1+a_2b_2+a_3b_3$. It gives a <i>number</i>. If it is $0$ the vectors are at right angles. Example: $(1,2)\\cdot(2,-1)=0$, so they are perpendicular."],
  [2, "It also gives angles: $a\\cdot b=|a||b|\\cos\\theta$. For $a=(1,2,2)$, $b=(2,0,1)$: $a\\cdot b=4$, so $\\cos\\theta=\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt5}$ and $\\theta\\approx53.4^\\circ$."],
  [3, "<b>Cross product</b> $a\\times b$ gives a <i>vector</i> perpendicular to both, with size $|a||b|\\sin\\theta$ (the area of the parallelogram). $(1,2,2)\\times(2,0,1)=(2,3,-4)$. It powers torque and magnetic force $F=qv\\times B$."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "For $a=(1,2,2)$ and $b=(2,0,1)$, $a\\cdot b=$", o: ["4", "5", "3", "6"], a: 0, e: "2 + 0 + 2 = 4." },
  { q: "Which pair is perpendicular?", o: ["(1,2) and (2,1)", "(1,2) and (2,-1)", "(3,1) and (1,3)", "(1,1) and (1,2)"], a: 1, e: "(1)(2) + (2)(-1) = 0." },
  { q: "The size of $(1,2,2)$ is:", o: ["5", "3", "√5", "9"], a: 1, e: "√(1+4+4) = 3." }] },

{ id: "ind", t: "6. Physics: electromagnetic induction", steps: [
  [1, "<b>Change is what matters.</b> A magnet sitting still next to a coil does nothing. A <i>moving</i> magnet, so the magnetic flux through the coil changes, pushes a current around it."],
  [2, "Flux $\\Phi=BA\\cos\\theta$. <b>Faraday</b>: $\\varepsilon=-N\\frac{\\Delta\\Phi}{\\Delta t}$. The emf is the rate of change of flux (the slope of a flux-time graph). Three ways to change flux: change $B$, change area $A$, or change the angle $\\theta$ (spin the coil: a generator)."],
  [2, "Example: 200-turn coil, flux falls from 0.050 Wb to 0.010 Wb in 0.10 s. $|\\varepsilon|=200\\times\\frac{0.040}{0.10}=80$ V."],
  [3, "<b>Lenz's law</b> (the minus sign): the induced current opposes the change in flux. If it helped the change, flux would grow, inducing more current in a runaway loop that makes energy from nothing. So Lenz's law is energy conservation. It explains why a magnet falls slowly down a copper tube."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "200 turns, flux changes by 0.040 Wb in 0.10 s. The emf magnitude is:", o: ["8 V", "80 V", "0.8 V", "800 V"], a: 1, e: "200 x 0.040 / 0.10 = 80 V." },
  { q: "A magnet is held still inside a coil. The induced emf is:", o: ["Zero, because flux is not changing", "Large", "Small but constant", "Depends on magnet strength only"], a: 0, e: "No change in flux means no emf, however strong the magnet." },
  { q: "Lenz's law says the induced current:", o: ["Helps the change in flux", "Opposes the change in flux", "Is always clockwise", "Is always zero"], a: 1, e: "It opposes the change, which conserves energy." }] }
];

COURSE.push({ id: "prob", t: "7. Probability and the normal curve", steps: [
  [1, "Probability = ways it can happen ÷ all equally likely outcomes, a number from 0 to 1. For independent events you multiply: two fair coins both heads is $\\frac12\\cdot\\frac12=\\frac14$."],
  [2, "<b>Binomial</b>: $n$ independent tries, each with success chance $p$. $P(X=k)=\\binom nk p^k(1-p)^{n-k}$, mean $np$. Five flips, exactly 2 heads: $\\binom52(0.5)^5=\\frac{10}{32}$."],
  [2, "<b>Normal curve</b>: the probability of a range is the <b>area</b> under the bell curve. $z=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}$ counts standard deviations from the mean. About 68%, 95%, 99.7% lie within 1, 2, 3 SDs. With $\\mu=100,\\sigma=15$, a score of 130 has $z=2$, so about 2.5% score higher."],
  [3, "<b>Why $\\sqrt n$?</b> Averages of $n$ measurements have spread $\\sigma/\\sqrt n$, so halving the uncertainty needs 4 times the data. A 95% interval for a proportion is $\\hat p\\pm1.96\\sqrt{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)/n}$."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "A fair coin is flipped 5 times. P(exactly 2 heads)?", o: ["10/32", "5/32", "1/4", "2/5"], a: 0, e: "C(5,2) = 10 ways out of 2^5 = 32." },
  { q: "$X$ is binomial with $n=20$, $p=0.3$. The mean is:", o: ["6", "14", "0.3", "20"], a: 0, e: "Mean = np = 20 x 0.3 = 6." },
  { q: "Scores have $\\mu=100$, $\\sigma=15$. The z-score of 70 is:", o: ["-2", "2", "-30", "-0.5"], a: 0, e: "(70 - 100)/15 = -2." },
  { q: "Roughly what percent of a normal distribution lies within 2 SDs of the mean?", o: ["95%", "68%", "99.7%", "50%"], a: 0, e: "The 68-95-99.7 rule." },
  { q: "To halve the width of a confidence interval you need a sample about:", o: ["4 times as big", "2 times as big", "half as big", "8 times as big"], a: 0, e: "Width goes like 1/√n, so halving it needs 4x the data." },
  { q: "A bag has 3 red and 2 blue balls. Two are drawn without replacement. P(both red)?", o: ["3/10", "9/25", "3/5", "1/5"], a: 0, e: "3/5 x 2/4 = 6/20 = 3/10." }] });

