// ===== More lessons (8-14) =====
COURSE.find(c => c.id === "vec").steps.push([3, "A <b>line in space</b> is $r=a+t\\,d$: a start point plus any multiple of a direction vector. Through $(1,2,3)$ with direction $(2,0,1)$: $(1+2t,\\;2,\\;3+t)$. Two lines meet only if one value of $t$ and one of $s$ satisfy all three coordinates at once."]);
COURSE.push(
{ id: "de", t: "8. Differential equations and the RC circuit", steps: [
  [1, "A <b>differential equation</b> says how fast something changes depends on its current size, e.g. $\\frac{dy}{dx}=2y$: the bigger it is, the faster it grows. The answer is a whole family of curves; a starting value (initial condition) picks one."],
  [2, "<b>Separable</b>: put $y$ with $dy$ and $x$ with $dx$, then integrate. $\\frac{dy}{dx}=ky\\Rightarrow\\int\\frac{dy}{y}=\\int k\\,dx\\Rightarrow y=Ae^{kx}$. With $y(0)=2$, $k=3$: $y=2e^{3x}$. $k>0$ is growth, $k<0$ is decay."],
  [2, "<b>Euler's method</b> steps along the slope: new $y$ = old $y$ + $h\\times$slope. For $\\frac{dy}{dx}=x+y$, $y(0)=1$, $h=0.1$: $y_1=1+0.1(0+1)=1.1$, then $y_2=1.1+0.1(0.1+1.1)=1.22$. Computers simulate physics this way."],
  [3, "<b>RC circuit</b>: charging obeys $RC\\frac{dV}{dt}=V_s-V$, solved by $V=V_s(1-e^{-t/RC})$. The <b>time constant</b> $\\tau=RC$: after $1\\tau$ the capacitor is 63% charged, after $5\\tau$ essentially full. The same maths describes cooling, radioactive decay and population growth."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "In $\\frac{dy}{dx}=ky$ with $k<0$ the solution:", o: ["decays exponentially", "grows exponentially", "is a straight line", "oscillates"], a: 0, e: "y = Ae^{kx} with negative k shrinks toward 0." },
  { q: "After $5\\tau$ a charging capacitor is about:", o: ["99% charged", "63% charged", "50% charged", "86% charged"], a: 0, e: "1 - e^{-5} ≈ 0.993." }] },
{ id: "mo", t: "9. Motion and projectiles", steps: [
  [1, "Motion graphs: the <b>slope</b> of position-time is velocity; the slope of velocity-time is acceleration; the <b>area</b> under velocity-time is distance travelled."],
  [2, "For <b>constant acceleration</b>: $v=u+at$, $s=ut+\\frac12at^2$, $v^2=u^2+2as$. A car from rest at $2\\,\\text{m/s}^2$ for 4 s reaches 8 m/s and covers $\\frac12(2)(16)=16$ m."],
  [2, "<b>Projectiles</b>: gravity acts only downward, so split into two problems sharing one time: horizontal at constant speed, vertical with $a=-g$ ($g=9.8$). At the top the vertical velocity is zero. Launch speed $v$ at angle $\\theta$: $v_x=v\\cos\\theta$, $v_y=v\\sin\\theta$."],
  [3, "On level ground: range $=\\frac{v^2\\sin2\\theta}{g}$ (largest at $45^\\circ$), flight time $=\\frac{2v\\sin\\theta}{g}$, max height $=\\frac{v_y^2}{2g}$. Check: 20 m/s at $30^\\circ$ gives 2.04 s, 35.3 m range, 5.1 m height."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "At the top of a projectile's path its:", o: ["vertical velocity is zero", "horizontal velocity is zero", "speed is zero", "acceleration is zero"], a: 0, e: "Gravity still acts, and the horizontal velocity never changes." },
  { q: "The area under a velocity-time graph gives:", o: ["distance travelled", "acceleration", "force", "time"], a: 0, e: "Velocity × time = distance." }] },
{ id: "fo", t: "10. Forces, circular motion and gravity", steps: [
  [1, "Newton's second law: <b>net force = mass × acceleration</b>. No net force means no change in velocity. Always draw a free-body diagram first."],
  [2, "On a slope of angle $\\theta$ the weight splits: $mg\\sin\\theta$ along the slope and $mg\\cos\\theta$ into it. Frictionless: $a=g\\sin\\theta$. With friction: $a=g(\\sin\\theta-\\mu\\cos\\theta)$."],
  [2, "<b>Circular motion</b>: direction changes, so there is always acceleration toward the centre: $a=\\frac{v^2}{r}=\\frac{4\\pi^2r}{T^2}$, net force $\\frac{mv^2}{r}$. 'Centripetal force' is whichever real force (tension, gravity, friction) points inward. There is no 'centrifugal force'."],
  [3, "<b>Gravity</b>: $F=\\frac{GMm}{r^2}$, so $g$ falls to a quarter at double the distance. Setting gravity equal to the centripetal force gives $T^2=\\frac{4\\pi^2}{GM}r^3$ (Kepler's third law); the satellite's mass cancels."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "An astronaut in orbit feels weightless because:", o: ["she is in free fall around Earth", "there is no gravity there", "she is outside Earth's field", "she moves too fast"], a: 0, e: "Gravity is still about 90% of surface strength; she and the station fall together." },
  { q: "A car turns on a flat road. The centripetal force comes from:", o: ["friction between tyres and road", "the engine", "a centrifugal force", "gravity"], a: 0, e: "It is the real inward force on the car." }] },
{ id: "el", t: "11. Electric and magnetic fields", steps: [
  [1, "A <b>field</b> is a map of the force a test charge would feel at each point. Electric field lines leave + and enter −. Between parallel plates the field is uniform: $E=\\frac Vd$, and the force on a charge is $F=qE$."],
  [2, "<b>Magnetic force</b> acts only on moving charges: $F=qvB\\sin\\theta$, at 90° to the velocity, so the path curves into a circle of radius $r=\\frac{mv}{qB}$. Use the right-hand rule. A proton at $10^6$ m/s in 0.5 T has $r\\approx2.1$ cm."],
  [2, "A magnetic force does <b>no work</b> (it is perpendicular to the motion), so it changes direction but never speed. An electric force can speed a charge up: $qV=\\frac12mv^2$."],
  [3, "A current-carrying wire in a field feels $F=nIlB$. A coil in a field has opposite forces on its two sides, giving the torque that drives a DC motor. A generator is the same device run backwards, linking to induction (lesson 6)."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "A magnetic field acting on a moving charge:", o: ["changes its direction but not its speed", "speeds it up", "slows it down", "has no effect"], a: 0, e: "Force is perpendicular to velocity, so no work is done." },
  { q: "Between parallel plates the electric field is:", o: ["uniform, E = V/d", "zero", "strongest at the edges", "E = Vd"], a: 0, e: "Equally spaced parallel field lines." }] },
{ id: "wv", t: "12. Waves, light and quantum", steps: [
  [1, "Waves carry energy without carrying matter: $v=f\\lambda$. When waves overlap they <b>interfere</b>: crest + crest is bigger (constructive); crest + trough cancels (destructive)."],
  [2, "<b>Double slit</b>: bright fringes where the path difference is a whole number of wavelengths; spacing $\\Delta x=\\frac{\\lambda L}{d}$. Standing waves on a string fixed at both ends: $f_n=\\frac{nv}{2L}$."],
  [2, "<b>Photoelectric effect</b>: light comes in photons of energy $E=hf$. An electron escapes only if $hf\\ge\\phi$, then $KE_{max}=hf-\\phi$. Brighter light gives more photons, not more energy per photon. $h=4.14\\times10^{-15}$ eV s."],
  [3, "<b>Wave-particle duality</b>: particles have wavelength $\\lambda=\\frac hp$. Electrons fired one at a time at a double slit still build up an interference pattern, so matter behaves like a wave too. Quantum mechanics is the theory that handles this."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "Brighter light (same frequency, above threshold) gives ejected electrons:", o: ["more of them, same maximum KE", "faster ones", "fewer of them", "no change at all"], a: 0, e: "More photons means more electrons; each photon's energy is unchanged." },
  { q: "Constructive interference happens when the path difference is:", o: ["a whole number of wavelengths", "half a wavelength", "zero only", "any value"], a: 0, e: "Crests then line up with crests." }] },
{ id: "re", t: "13. Special relativity", steps: [
  [1, "Two ideas: the laws of physics are the same in every non-accelerating frame, and the speed of light is the same for everyone. So <b>time and length are not absolute</b>."],
  [2, "$\\gamma=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}}$. <b>Time dilation</b>: $t=\\gamma t_0$ ($t_0$ is the proper time, measured on the moving clock itself). <b>Length contraction</b>: $L=\\frac{L_0}{\\gamma}$. At $0.8c$, $\\gamma=1.67$."],
  [2, "$E=mc^2$: mass is stored energy. 1 g of matter is about $9\\times10^{13}$ J. Muons made high in the atmosphere reach the ground because their clocks run slow in our frame."],
  [3, "<b>Derive it</b>: in a light clock the moving observer sees a diagonal path, so $(ct/2)^2=(vt/2)^2+(ct_0/2)^2$ and $t=\\frac{t_0}{\\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}}=\\gamma t_0$. Just Pythagoras plus a constant $c$."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "Proper time is measured by:", o: ["a clock at rest relative to both events", "a clock moving past the events", "any observer", "the fastest observer"], a: 0, e: "It is the time on the clock that is present at both events." },
  { q: "As $v\\to c$, $\\gamma$:", o: ["becomes very large", "goes to zero", "stays at 1", "becomes negative"], a: 0, e: "The denominator √(1 - v²/c²) goes to 0." }] },
{ id: "py", t: "14. Python and algorithms", steps: [
  [1, "Python reads like English. <code>for i in range(3): print(i)</code> prints 0, 1, 2: computers count from <b>0</b>, and <code>range(a,b)</code> never reaches b. Indentation groups the lines inside the loop."],
  [2, "Functions are reusable machines: <code>def area(r): return 3.14159*r**2</code>. Lists hold ordered values (<code>[72,85,90]</code>); dictionaries look things up by name. <code>%</code> gives the remainder: <code>17 % 5</code> is 2."],
  [2, "<b>Binary</b> is base 2 (128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1): $45=32+8+4+1=101101_2$. <b>Hex</b> is base 16 (0-9, A-F), one hex digit = four bits: $45=\\text{0x2D}$. Logic gates (AND, OR, XOR, NOT) are what Systems Engineering circuits and CPUs are built from."],
  [3, "<b>Binary search</b>: in a sorted list check the middle and discard half each time, so $n$ items need about $\\log_2 n$ checks (a million items: about 20). <b>Recursion</b> is a function that calls itself on a smaller problem, with a base case. The right algorithm often beats faster hardware."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "Binary <code>1010</code> in decimal is:", o: ["10", "12", "8", "20"], a: 0, e: "8 + 2 = 10." },
  { q: "Why does <code>range(5)</code> stop at 4?", o: ["It counts from 0 and excludes the end value", "It is a bug", "Lists start at 1", "5 is reserved"], a: 0, e: "range(5) gives 0, 1, 2, 3, 4: five numbers." }] }
);

