// ===== Module 4 and 5 lessons: first-year engineering and CS content =====
COURSE.push(
{ id: "ci", t: "Circuits for engineers: Ohm, Kirchhoff and dividers", steps: [
  [1, "Circuits move charge. <b>Voltage</b> $V$ is the push, <b>current</b> $I$ the flow, <b>resistance</b> $R$ the opposition. <b>Ohm's law</b>: $V=IR$. Power: $P=VI=I^2R$. A 12 V supply across 4 Ω drives 3 A and dissipates 36 W."],
  [2, "<b>Series</b> resistors add, $R=R_1+R_2$, and share one current. <b>Parallel</b>: $\\frac1R=\\frac1{R_1}+\\frac1{R_2}$, sharing one voltage. 6 Ω in parallel with 3 Ω gives $\\frac{6\\times3}{6+3}=2\\,\\Omega$."],
  [2, "<b>Kirchhoff</b>: currents into a junction sum to zero (KCL); voltages around a loop sum to zero (KVL). A <b>voltage divider</b> follows: $V_{out}=V_{in}\\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}$. 9 V across 1 kΩ then 2 kΩ gives 6 V across the 2 kΩ."],
  [3, "At uni you solve bigger circuits by writing KCL/KVL as simultaneous equations and solving with <b>matrices</b>, or by <b>nodal analysis</b>. Add capacitors and you get differential equations ($\\tau=RC$); add AC and you use complex numbers (impedance $Z=R+jX$). This is where Modules 1 and 2 pay off."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "At a junction 3 A and 2 A flow in, and 4 A flows out of one wire. The other wire carries:", o: ["1 A out", "5 A out", "9 A out", "0 A"], a: 0, e: "KCL: in = out, so 5 A in means 5 A out in total; 4 A leaves one wire, 1 A the other." },
  { q: "Two resistors in parallel have an equivalent resistance that is:", o: ["less than the smaller one", "greater than the larger one", "their sum", "their average"], a: 0, e: "More paths for current means less total opposition." }] },
{ id: "st", t: "Statics: forces and moments", steps: [
  [1, "<b>Statics</b> asks why a bridge, crane or shelf doesn't move. Because it is in <b>equilibrium</b>: the forces balance ($\\sum F=0$) and so do the turning effects ($\\sum M=0$)."],
  [2, "A <b>moment</b> (torque) is force × perpendicular distance from the pivot: $M=Fd$. A 50 N force 0.4 m from a bolt gives 20 N m. A seesaw balances when $F_1d_1=F_2d_2$: 30 kg at 2 m balances 20 kg at 3 m."],
  [2, "<b>Beam problem</b>: a 6 m beam rests on supports at each end with a 600 N load 2 m from the left. Take moments about the left support: $R_B\\times6=600\\times2$, so $R_B=200$ N and $R_A=600-200=400$ N."],
  [3, "Forces are vectors, so a cable at angle $\\theta$ carries a vertical load through its vertical component: $T\\sin\\theta=W$. Engineers resolve forces into components (the vectors lesson) and solve the equations. Trusses, bridges and cranes in first-year mechanics are these same equations, scaled up."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "A structure in equilibrium has net force and net moment:", o: ["both zero", "force zero only", "moment zero only", "both equal to its weight"], a: 0, e: "It neither accelerates nor rotates." },
  { q: "Moving a force of the same size further from the pivot makes its moment:", o: ["larger", "smaller", "unchanged", "zero"], a: 0, e: "M = Fd grows with d." }] },
{ id: "dm", t: "Discrete maths: logic, sets, counting and induction", steps: [
  [1, "CS runs on <b>logic</b>. A proposition is true or false. AND, OR, NOT combine them. $p\\Rightarrow q$ ('if p then q') is false only when p is true and q is false. XOR is true when exactly one input is true."],
  [2, "<b>Sets</b>: $A\\cup B$ (in A or B), $A\\cap B$ (in both). $|A\\cup B|=|A|+|B|-|A\\cap B|$: with sizes 8, 5 and 3 in common, the union has 10. <b>De Morgan</b>: $\\neg(p\\land q)=\\neg p\\lor\\neg q$."],
  [2, "<b>Counting</b>: $n!$ orderings of $n$ items; choose $k$ of $n$: $\\binom nk=\\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}$. 5 books in a row: 120 ways; a team of 3 from 8: 56 ways. Binary strings of length $n$: $2^n$."],
  [3, "<b>Induction</b> proves a statement for all $n$, which is how you prove loops and recursive functions correct: show the base case, then show that if it holds for $k$ it holds for $k+1$. Example: $1+2+\\dots+n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$. Modular arithmetic ($17\\bmod5=2$) underlies hashing and encryption."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "By De Morgan, $\\neg(p\\land q)$ equals:", o: ["¬p ∨ ¬q", "¬p ∧ ¬q", "p ∨ q", "¬p ∨ q"], a: 0, e: "Negating an AND flips it to an OR of the negations." },
  { q: "A proof by induction needs:", o: ["a base case and an inductive step", "only a base case", "many worked examples", "a counterexample"], a: 0, e: "The base case starts the dominoes; the step keeps them falling." }] },
{ id: "ds", t: "Data structures, sorting and Big-O", steps: [
  [1, "A <b>data structure</b> organises data so operations are fast. A <b>list/array</b> gives instant access by position. A <b>stack</b> is last-in-first-out (the undo button); a <b>queue</b> is first-in-first-out (print jobs); a <b>dictionary</b> finds a value by its key in about one step."],
  [2, "<b>Big-O</b> describes how work grows with input size $n$: $O(1)$ constant, $O(\\log n)$ halving, $O(n)$ one pass, $O(n^2)$ a loop inside a loop, $O(2^n)$ explosive. Searching an unsorted list is $O(n)$; binary search on a sorted list is $O(\\log n)$."],
  [2, "<b>Sorting</b>: bubble sort repeatedly swaps neighbours, $O(n^2)$. Merge sort splits the list, sorts each half recursively, then merges, $O(n\\log n)$. For $n=1000$ that is about 10,000 steps versus 1,000,000."],
  [3, "<b>Recursion and trees</b>: a binary tree node has up to two children, and searching a balanced tree takes $O(\\log n)$ because each step discards half. In first-year CS you will implement these in code and prove them correct with induction (previous lesson). Check the Monash Handbook for the exact units."]],
  qs: [
  { q: "Which structure is last-in, first-out?", o: ["stack", "queue", "array", "dictionary"], a: 0, e: "Think of a stack of plates." },
  { q: "Merge sort runs in:", o: ["O(n log n)", "O(n²)", "O(n)", "O(log n)"], a: 0, e: "log n levels of splitting, n work per level." }] }
);
COURSE.slice(-4).forEach(c => { c.base = c.qs.slice(); });
