const WP = {
fn: "Find the inverse of $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{x-3}$ and state its domain. Write $y=\\frac{2x+1}{x-3}$ and swap: $x=\\frac{2y+1}{y-3}$. Multiply out: $xy-3x=2y+1$. Collect the $y$ terms: $y(x-2)=3x+1$. So $f^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{x-2}$ with $x\\ne2$. Check: $f(4)=9$ and $f^{-1}(9)=\\frac{28}{7}=4$ ✓.",
chain: "A can must hold 500 cm³. Find the radius that minimises the surface area $A=2\\pi r^2+2\\pi rh$. Eliminate $h$: $h=\\frac{500}{\\pi r^2}$, so $A=2\\pi r^2+\\frac{1000}{r}$. Differentiate: $A'=4\\pi r-\\frac{1000}{r^2}=0$, so $r^3=\\frac{250}{\\pi}$ and $r\\approx4.30$ cm, $h\\approx8.61$ cm (about $2r$). $A''>0$, so it is a minimum.",
int: "Find the area enclosed by $y=x^2$ and $y=2x$. Intersections: $x^2=2x$ gives $x=0$ and $x=2$. Between them $2x$ is on top. Area $=\\int_0^2(2x-x^2)\\,dx=\\left[x^2-\\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_0^2=4-\\frac83=\\frac43$.",
cx: "Solve $z^2-2z+5=0$. Formula: $z=\\frac{2\\pm\\sqrt{4-20}}{2}=\\frac{2\\pm4i}{2}=1\\pm2i$. Check: sum of roots $=2$ ✓ and product $(1+2i)(1-2i)=1+4=5$ ✓. Roots of a real polynomial come in conjugate pairs.",
vec: "Find the angle and parallelogram area for $a=(2,-1,2)$ and $b=(1,2,2)$. Dot: $a\\cdot b=2-2+4=4$. Sizes: $|a|=|b|=3$. So $\\cos\\theta=\\frac49$ and $\\theta\\approx63.6^\\circ$. Cross: $a\\times b=(-6,-2,5)$, so the area is $\\sqrt{36+4+25}=\\sqrt{65}\\approx8.06$.",
ind: "A 50-turn square coil (side 0.10 m) sits in a 0.40 T field perpendicular to it and is pulled out of the field in 0.20 s. Area $=0.01$ m², so the flux falls from $0.40\\times0.01=0.004$ Wb to 0. $|\\varepsilon|=50\\times\\frac{0.004}{0.20}=1.0$ V. Direction (Lenz): if the field points into the page and is decreasing, the induced current makes a field into the page too, so it flows <b>clockwise</b>.",
prob: "A machine makes 5% faulty items. In a batch of 20, find P(at least one faulty). Use the complement: $P(\\text{none})=0.95^{20}\\approx0.358$, so $P(\\ge1)=1-0.358=0.642$. 'At least one' almost always means 'one minus none'.",
de: "A drink at 90 °C sits in a 20 °C room with $\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-20)$. After 5 min it is 70 °C. Find $T$ at 10 min. Let $u=T-20$: $u=70e^{-kt}$. At $t=5$: $50=70e^{-5k}$, so $e^{-5k}=\\frac57$. At $t=10$: $u=70\\left(\\frac57\\right)^2=35.7$, so $T\\approx55.7$ °C.",
mo: "A ball is thrown horizontally at 15 m/s from a 20 m cliff. Vertical: $20=\\frac12(9.8)t^2$ gives $t=2.02$ s. Horizontal distance $=15\\times2.02=30.3$ m. Impact: $v_y=9.8\\times2.02=19.8$ m/s, so speed $=\\sqrt{15^2+19.8^2}=24.8$ m/s at $\\arctan\\frac{19.8}{15}=52.9^\\circ$ below horizontal.",
fo: "A 1200 kg car rounds a flat bend of radius 50 m with $\\mu=0.8$. Friction provides the centripetal force: $\\mu mg=\\frac{mv^2}{r}$, so $v=\\sqrt{\\mu gr}=\\sqrt{0.8\\times9.8\\times50}=19.8$ m/s (about 71 km/h). The mass cancels, so a heavier car has the same limit.",
el: "An electron is accelerated through 400 V and then enters a 2.0 mT field at right angles. Speed: $v=\\sqrt{\\frac{2qV}{m}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{2(1.6\\times10^{-19})(400)}{9.11\\times10^{-31}}}=1.19\\times10^7$ m/s. Radius: $r=\\frac{mv}{qB}=\\frac{(9.11\\times10^{-31})(1.19\\times10^7)}{(1.6\\times10^{-19})(0.002)}=0.034$ m, about 3.4 cm.",
wv: "Light of wavelength 450 nm hits a metal with threshold frequency $5.0\\times10^{14}$ Hz. Work function: $\\phi=hf_0=4.14\\times10^{-15}\\times5.0\\times10^{14}=2.07$ eV. Photon energy: $E=\\frac{1240}{450}=2.76$ eV (using $hc=1240$ eV nm). $KE_{max}=2.76-2.07=0.69$ eV, so the stopping voltage is 0.69 V.",
re: "A ship passes at $0.6c$ ($\\gamma=1.25$). Its crew measure its length as 80 m (proper length). In our frame $L=\\frac{80}{1.25}=64$ m. If the crew's clock shows 12 s between two events on the ship (proper time), we measure $t=1.25\\times12=15$ s. Each answer uses the proper quantity in the right place.",
py: "Write a function returning the average of the positive numbers in a list, or 0 if there are none. <code>def avg_pos(xs): ps=[x for x in xs if x&gt;0]; return sum(ps)/len(ps) if ps else 0</code>. Test: <code>avg_pos([3,-1,5,0])</code> keeps 3 and 5, giving 4.0. Always test the empty and all-negative cases too.",
ci: "A 12 V source drives $R_1=100\\,\\Omega$ in series with $R_2=300\\,\\Omega$ in parallel with $R_3=600\\,\\Omega$. Parallel pair: $\\frac{300\\times600}{900}=200\\,\\Omega$. Total $=300\\,\\Omega$, so $I=\\frac{12}{300}=40$ mA. $V_{R1}=4$ V, so the pair has 8 V: $I_2=26.7$ mA and $I_3=13.3$ mA, which add to 40 mA ✓.",
st: "A 3 m uniform plank (100 N) rests on supports at A (left end) and B (2 m from A). A 600 N person stands at the right end. Moments about A: $R_B\\times2=100\\times1.5+600\\times3=1950$, so $R_B=975$ N. Vertical balance: $R_A=700-975=-275$ N. A negative reaction means the support at A would have to pull <i>down</i>: the plank tips unless it is tied.",
dm: "Prove $n^2+n$ is even for every integer $n$. Factorise: $n^2+n=n(n+1)$, a product of two consecutive integers, and one of them is even, so the product is even. Counting: 3-letter codes from A-Z with no repeats: $26\\times25\\times24=15{,}600$.",
ds: "Compare searching 1,000,000 sorted items. Linear search: up to 1,000,000 checks. Binary search: $\\lceil\\log_2 1{,}000{,}000\\rceil=20$ checks. At 1 µs per check that is 1 s against 20 µs. Double the list to 2 million: binary search needs just 1 more check; linear search needs twice as many.",
la: "Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of $A$ with rows $(4,\\,1)$ and $(2,\\,3)$. $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=(4-\\lambda)(3-\\lambda)-2=\\lambda^2-7\\lambda+10=(\\lambda-5)(\\lambda-2)$, so $\\lambda=5,2$. For $\\lambda=5$: $-v_1+v_2=0$, so $v=(1,1)$. For $\\lambda=2$: $2v_1+v_2=0$, so $v=(1,-2)$. Check: $A(1,1)=(5,5)$ ✓.",
ta: "Find the series of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{1-x}$ and estimate $\\frac1{0.9}$. It is a geometric series: $1+x+x^2+x^3+\\cdots$ for $|x|<1$. With $x=0.1$: $1+0.1+0.01+0.001=1.111$ (true value 1.1111). The series fails for $|x|\\ge1$: that is its radius of convergence.",
mv: "A current $I=V/R$ is measured with $V=12\\pm0.1$ V and $R=100\\pm1\\,\\Omega$. $I=0.12$ A. $\\frac{\\partial I}{\\partial V}=\\frac1R=0.01$ and $\\frac{\\partial I}{\\partial R}=-\\frac{V}{R^2}=-0.0012$. Worst-case error: $\\Delta I\\approx0.01(0.1)+0.0012(1)=0.0022$ A, so $I=0.120\\pm0.002$ A.",
sq: "Prove by induction that $1+3+5+\\cdots+(2n-1)=n^2$. Base $n=1$: $1=1$ ✓. Step: assume true for $k$. Then add the next term: $k^2+(2k+1)=(k+1)^2$ ✓. Also: a geometric series with first term 3 and ratio $\\frac12$ sums to $\\frac{3}{1-1/2}=6$.",
ci2: "A non-inverting amplifier has $R_f=47$ kΩ and $R_{in}=10$ kΩ with a 0.2 V input: gain $=1+\\frac{47}{10}=5.7$, so $V_{out}=1.14$ V. Thévenin: a 10 V source with 2 kΩ in series feeds a 3 kΩ resistor to ground. $V_{th}=10\\times\\frac{3}{5}=6$ V and $R_{th}=2\\parallel3=1.2$ kΩ.",
st2: "A 2 m uniform beam (80 N) is hinged at a wall and held horizontal by a cable at 30° to the beam at its free end, which also carries a 40 N load. Moments about the hinge: $T\\sin30^\\circ\\times2=80\\times1+40\\times2=160$, so $T=160$ N. Hinge forces: horizontal $=T\\cos30^\\circ=138.6$ N; vertical $=120-T\\sin30^\\circ=40$ N upward.",
sg: "Design an RC low-pass filter with a 1 kHz cut-off. Pick $C=100$ nF: $R=\\frac{1}{2\\pi fC}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi(1000)(10^{-7})}=1.59$ kΩ (use 1.6 kΩ). At 10 kHz the gain is $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+(f/f_c)^2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{101}}\\approx0.0995$, about −20 dB."
};
COURSE.forEach(c => { if (WP[c.id] && !c.hasWP) { c.steps.splice(c.steps.length - 1, 0, [2, "<b>✍️ Worked problem</b> (try it on paper first, then check). " + WP[c.id]]); c.hasWP = 1; } });

// ===== Rewritten lessons: smaller steps, plainer language, analogies first, quick checks, then the deeper "why" =====
const REW = (id, steps) => { COURSE.find(c => c.id === id).steps = steps; };
const WPS = id => "<b>✍️ Worked problem</b> (try it on paper first, then check). " + WP[id];
REW("fn", [
 [1, "<b>Start with a vending machine.</b> You press a button (the <b>input</b>) and one snack drops out (the <b>output</b>). A <b>function</b> is exactly that: a rule that turns each input into <i>exactly one</i> output.<br><br>$f(x)=2x+1$ is the rule 'double the input, then add 1'. Put in 3: $2(3)+1=7$. We write $f(3)=7$.", null, ["What is $f(5)$ if $f(x)=2x+1$?", "$2(5)+1=11$."]],
 [1, "<b>Domain and range</b> just mean 'what can go in' and 'what can come out'.<br><br>Ask two questions: <b>1)</b> What would break the machine? (dividing by 0, square-rooting a negative, log of 0 or less). Those inputs are not allowed. <b>2)</b> What can it actually produce?<br><br>Example: $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ breaks for negative $x$, so the <b>domain</b> is $x\\ge0$. It never outputs a negative, so the <b>range</b> is $y\\ge0$.", null, ["What is the domain of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-2}$?", "Every number except $x=2$, because that would mean dividing by zero."]],
 [2, "<b>Moving a graph: one change at a time.</b> Start with $y=x^2$, a U-shape with its bottom at $(0,0)$.<br><br>• $y=x^2+3$: every output gets 3 bigger, so the graph moves <b>up 3</b>.<br>• $y=(x-2)^2$: the machine needs an input 2 bigger to give the same output, so the graph moves <b>right 2</b>. (It feels backwards, and that is the classic trap.)<br>• $y=2x^2$: outputs doubled, so the graph is <b>taller</b>.<br>• $y=-x^2$: outputs flipped, so it is <b>upside down</b>.<br><br><b>Rule of thumb:</b> changes <i>inside</i> the bracket (touching $x$) act sideways and 'backwards'. Changes <i>outside</i> act up/down as you would expect.", "transform", ["Where is the bottom of $y=(x+1)^2-4$?", "$(-1,-4)$: the $+1$ inside moves it left 1, the $-4$ outside moves it down 4."]],
 [2, "<b>Composite functions: a production line.</b> $f(g(x))$ means: send $x$ into machine $g$ first, then feed <i>g's output</i> into machine $f$.<br><br>Let $g(x)=x+3$ (add 3) and $f(x)=x^2$ (square it).<br>$f(g(2))$: $g(2)=5$, then $f(5)=25$.<br>$g(f(2))$: $f(2)=4$, then $g(4)=7$.<br><br><b>The order matters.</b> As a formula, $f(g(x))=(x+3)^2$: replace every $x$ in $f$ with the whole of $g$.", null, ["$f(x)=3x$ and $g(x)=x-1$. Find $f(g(4))$.", "$g(4)=3$, then $f(3)=9$."]],
 [2, "<b>Inverse functions: the undo machine.</b> If $f$ turns 4 into 8, the inverse $f^{-1}$ turns 8 back into 4.<br><br><b>Recipe:</b> (1) write $y=f(x)$. (2) <b>Swap</b> $x$ and $y$. (3) Solve for $y$.<br><br>For $f(x)=3x-4$: swap to get $x=3y-4$, so $y=\\frac{x+4}{3}$.<br>Check it: $f(4)=8$, and $f^{-1}(8)=\\frac{12}{3}=4$ ✓. Always run this check.", "inverse", ["Find the inverse of $f(x)=\\frac{x}{2}+1$.", "Swap: $x=\\frac y2+1$, so $y=2(x-1)=2x-2$."]],
 [2, "<b>Why only some functions have inverses.</b> An undo machine must give <i>one</i> answer. But $f(x)=x^2$ turns both 3 and $-3$ into 9, so 'undoing 9' is ambiguous.<br><br><b>Fix:</b> restrict the domain to $x\\ge0$. Now $f^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{x}$.<br><b>Horizontal line test:</b> if any horizontal line crosses the graph twice, there is no inverse.<br><b>Swap rule:</b> the domain of $f$ becomes the range of $f^{-1}$, and the range becomes the domain."],
 [2, WPS("fn")],
 [3, "<b>Deeper: logs are the undo button for powers.</b> $2^x$ and $\\log_2 x$ are inverses, so $\\log_2(2^x)=x$ and $2^{\\log_2 x}=x$. Their graphs are mirror images in $y=x$.<br><br>This is how you rescue a variable stuck in an exponent: to solve $2^{x+1}=32$ ask '2 to what power is 32?' (5), so $x+1=5$ and $x=4$. The same idea runs decibels, pH, earthquake scales and algorithm speeds."]
]);
REW("chain", [
 [1, "<b>The speedometer idea.</b> Drive 60 km in 1 hour and your <i>average</i> speed is 60 km/h. But the speedometer shows your speed <b>right now</b>.<br><br>The <b>derivative</b> is the speedometer reading for any graph: the <b>slope at one exact point</b>. Steep uphill = big positive. Flat = 0. Downhill = negative.", null, ["What is the slope at the very top of a smooth hill?", "0: at the top the curve is momentarily flat."]],
 [1, "<b>Slope needs two points, so we cheat.</b> Pick a second point very close by and slide it closer and closer. The line through the two points (a <i>secant</i>) turns into the <i>tangent</i>. The tangent's slope is the derivative, written $f'(x)$ or $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ ('a tiny change in $y$ per tiny change in $x$').", "tangent"],
 [2, "<b>The power rule in plain words:</b> <b>bring the power down the front, then take 1 off the power.</b><br><br>$x^3\\to3x^2$, $\\;x^5\\to5x^4$, $\\;x\\to1$, a plain number $\\to0$.<br>Numbers in front just come along: $4x^3\\to12x^2$. Add pieces separately: $x^2+5x\\to2x+5$.<br><br>Check: slope of $y=x^3$ at $x=2$ is $3(2)^2=12$.", null, ["Differentiate $y=4x^2-7x+1$.", "$y'=8x-7$."]],
 [2, "<b>Four more to memorise:</b> $(e^x)'=e^x$ (its slope always equals its height), $(\\ln x)'=\\frac1x$, $(\\sin x)'=\\cos x$, $(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x$.<br><br>Rhyme: 'sin goes to cos, cos goes to <i>minus</i> sin'."],
 [2, "<b>The chain rule is a gear train.</b> $y=\\sin(3x)$ is two machines in a row: first $u=3x$, then $y=\\sin u$.<br><br>If $u$ changes 3 times as fast as $x$, and $y$ changes $\\cos u$ times as fast as $u$, then $y$ changes $3\\cos u$ times as fast as $x$. <b>Multiply the rates:</b> $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{dy}{du}\\cdot\\frac{du}{dx}$.<br><br>In words: <b>derivative of the outside (inside left alone) × derivative of the inside.</b>", "chain", ["Differentiate $(5x+2)^4$.", "$4(5x+2)^3\\cdot5=20(5x+2)^3$."]],
 [2, "<b>A 3-step routine you can always follow.</b> Try $y=e^{x^2}$.<br><br><b>1.</b> Spot inside and outside: inside $x^2$, outside $e^{\\square}$.<br><b>2.</b> Differentiate the outside, copying the inside: $e^{x^2}$.<br><b>3.</b> Multiply by the derivative of the inside, $2x$.<br><br>Answer: $2x\\,e^{x^2}$.<br><br><b>Most common mistake:</b> forgetting step 3.", null, ["Differentiate $\\cos(4x)$.", "$-4\\sin(4x)$: outside gives $-\\sin(4x)$, inside gives 4."]],
 [2, "<b>Product and quotient rules</b> for when two pieces are multiplied or divided.<br><br>Product: $(uv)'=u'v+uv'$. Say: 'derivative of the first times the second, plus the first times the derivative of the second'. So $x^2\\sin x\\to2x\\sin x+x^2\\cos x$.<br><br>Quotient: $\\left(\\frac uv\\right)'=\\frac{u'v-uv'}{v^2}$ (the order on top matters)."],
 [2, WPS("chain")],
 [3, "<b>Deeper: why does the derivative find peaks?</b> At the top of a hill you are going neither up nor down, so the slope is 0. Solve $f'(x)=0$ for candidates, then check $f''$: positive means a valley (smile), negative a peak (frown).<br><br><b>Where does the power rule come from?</b> For $x^2$ the slope between $x$ and $x+h$ is $\\frac{(x+h)^2-x^2}{h}=2x+h$. Let $h\\to0$ and you get $2x$. Every rule is proved by this 'let the gap shrink to zero' idea."]
]);
REW("int", [
 [1, "<b>The odometer idea.</b> A speedometer tells your speed; the <b>odometer</b> tells the total distance. Integration goes from a rate to a total. On a graph, it is the <b>area under the curve</b>.<br><br>Constant 10 m/s for 5 s: the area is a rectangle, $10\\times5=50$ m.", null, ["Constant speed 4 m/s for 6 s. Distance?", "$4\\times6=24$ m, the area of a rectangle."]],
 [1, "<b>When the speed changes,</b> chop time into thin strips. Each strip is almost a rectangle (height × width), so add them up. The thinner the strips, the more accurate the total.<br><br>The limit of that sum is the <b>definite integral</b> $\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx$. The $\\int$ sign is a stretched 'S' for 'sum'.", "area"],
 [2, "<b>Antiderivatives: differentiating backwards.</b> Since $(x^3)'=3x^2$, an antiderivative of $3x^2$ is $x^3$.<br><br><b>Rule:</b> raise the power by 1, then divide by the new power: $\\int x^n\\,dx=\\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}+c$.<br>So $\\int x^2\\,dx=\\frac{x^3}{3}+c$.<br><br>The $+c$ is there because constants vanish when you differentiate, so we cannot know which one was there.", null, ["Find $\\int 6x^2\\,dx$.", "$2x^3+c$."]],
 [2, "<b>The Fundamental Theorem is the shortcut.</b> To find the area from $a$ to $b$: find an antiderivative $F$, then compute $F(b)-F(a)$.<br><br>$\\int_0^3 2x\\,dx$: $F=x^2$, so $3^2-0^2=9$. Sanity check: a triangle with base 3 and height 6 has area 9 ✓.<br>No $+c$ needed: it cancels in $F(b)-F(a)$.", null, ["Evaluate $\\int_0^2 3x^2\\,dx$.", "$[x^3]_0^2=8-0=8$."]],
 [2, "<b>Integrals with an inside (the chain rule backwards).</b> Differentiating $e^{2x}$ gives an extra factor 2. So when integrating we <b>divide by the inside's slope</b>:<br>$\\int e^{2x}dx=\\frac12e^{2x}+c$, $\\quad\\int\\cos(3x)\\,dx=\\frac13\\sin(3x)+c$.<br><br><b>Always check:</b> differentiate your answer. You must get the original function back.", null, ["Find $\\int\\sin(2x)\\,dx$.", "$-\\frac12\\cos(2x)+c$."]],
 [2, "<b>Area between two curves = top minus bottom.</b> For $y=x$ and $y=x^2$ from 0 to 1, the line is on top: $\\int_0^1(x-x^2)dx=\\frac12-\\frac13=\\frac16$.<br><br><b>Traps:</b> (1) choosing the wrong curve as 'top'. (2) A region <i>below</i> the x-axis gives a <i>negative</i> integral, so treat that part separately and take its positive value for area.", "between"],
 [2, WPS("int")],
 [3, "<b>Deeper: why do 'area' and 'undoing the derivative' match?</b> Let $A(x)$ be the area so far. Adding a sliver of width $h$ adds about $f(x)\\cdot h$, so $\\frac{\\Delta A}{h}\\approx f(x)$. The <i>rate</i> at which area grows is the height of the curve: $A'(x)=f(x)$.<br><br>So 'area so far' is an antiderivative of $f$. That is the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, and it is why one simple subtraction replaces adding infinitely many strips."]
]);
REW("prob", [
 [1, "<b>Probability is a fraction:</b> (ways it can happen) ÷ (all equally likely outcomes). A die: P(even) = 3/6 = 1/2. It always sits between 0 (impossible) and 1 (certain).<br><br><b>'Not' rule:</b> P(not A) = 1 − P(A). Handy for 'at least one' questions.", null, ["P(rolling more than 4 on a die)?", "Two outcomes (5, 6) out of 6: 1/3."]],
 [1, "<b>'And' multiplies, 'or' adds (carefully).</b> If two events do not affect each other (<b>independent</b>), P(both) = P(A) × P(B). Two heads: ½ × ½ = ¼.<br><br>For 'A or B' when both could happen: P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B), so the overlap is not counted twice.<br>A <b>tree diagram</b> lays out the stages: multiply along branches, add the endings you want."],
 [2, "<b>Binomial: counting successes.</b> Use it when there is a fixed number $n$ of tries, each success/failure, the same chance $p$ every time, and tries are independent.<br><br>$P(X=k)=\\binom nk p^k(1-p)^{n-k}$. Read it as: (ways to choose which $k$ tries succeed) × ($p$ for each success) × ($1-p$ for each failure).<br>5 flips, exactly 2 heads: $\\binom52(0.5)^2(0.5)^3=\\frac{10}{32}$.<br>Mean $=np$. Spread $=\\sqrt{np(1-p)}$.", null, ["10 flips of a fair coin: expected number of heads?", "$np=10\\times0.5=5$."]],
 [2, "<b>Continuous data needs curves.</b> A height can be 172.3841... cm, so the chance of any <i>exact</i> value is 0. Instead we ask for the chance of a <i>range</i>, and that equals the <b>area</b> under a curve. The total area is 1.<br><br>(This is where integration comes back!)"],
 [2, "<b>The normal curve.</b> A bell shape, centred on the mean $\\mu$, with width set by the standard deviation $\\sigma$. About <b>68%</b> of values lie within 1 SD of the mean, <b>95%</b> within 2, and <b>99.7%</b> within 3.", "normal", ["Mean 100, SD 10. About what percent lie between 80 and 120?", "95%, because that is within 2 SDs."]],
 [2, "<b>z-scores are a common ruler.</b> $z=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}$ counts how many SDs a value is from the mean. Mean 100, SD 15, score 130: $z=2$, so only about 2.5% score higher.<br><br>Positive $z$ = above average, negative = below. On your calculator use normalCDF(lower, upper, μ, σ) for areas and invNorm to go backwards from a probability.", null, ["Mean 50, SD 5. What is $z$ for $x=40$?", "$z=\\frac{40-50}{5}=-2$."]],
 [2, WPS("prob")],
 [3, "<b>Deeper: why sample size matters.</b> Averages of $n$ measurements wobble less than single values: their SD is $\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}$. To <i>halve</i> the error you need <b>4 times</b> the data.<br><br>A 95% confidence interval for a proportion is $\\hat p\\pm1.96\\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)}{n}}$: 'our estimate, give or take about two standard errors'.", "sampling"]
]);

// ===== New lessons: circular functions, calculus of sin/cos/tan/e, thermal physics =====
COURSE.push(
{ id: "tr", t: "Circular functions: sin, cos, tan and the unit circle", steps: [
 [1, "<b>Think of a Ferris wheel.</b> As it turns, your height goes up and down in a smooth repeating wave. Sine and cosine are the maths of that wave: <b>one full turn of the wheel = one full wave</b>.", null, ["How many full waves does one full turn make?", "One."]],
 [1, "<b>The unit circle</b> is a circle of radius 1. Turn through an angle $\\theta$ from the right-hand side and you are at the point $(\\cos\\theta,\\sin\\theta)$. So <b>cos is the sideways (x) position and sin is the height (y)</b>. At the top (90°) you are at $(0,1)$: $\\sin90^\\circ=1$, $\\cos90^\\circ=0$.", "unitcircle", ["What are $\\sin0$ and $\\cos0$?", "$\\sin0=0$ and $\\cos0=1$: you start at the rightmost point $(1,0)$."]],
 [1, "<b>Radians</b> measure an angle by the length of arc it cuts off on the unit circle. A full turn is $2\\pi$ (the circumference), a half turn is $\\pi$, a quarter turn is $\\frac\\pi2$.<br><br>To convert degrees to radians multiply by $\\frac{\\pi}{180}$: $60^\\circ=\\frac\\pi3$.", "radian", ["Convert $45^\\circ$ to radians.", "$45\\times\\frac{\\pi}{180}=\\frac\\pi4$."]],
 [2, "<b>Exact values to remember.</b><br>$\\sin\\frac\\pi6=\\frac12,\\ \\cos\\frac\\pi6=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$<br>$\\sin\\frac\\pi4=\\cos\\frac\\pi4=\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}$<br>$\\sin\\frac\\pi3=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2},\\ \\cos\\frac\\pi3=\\frac12$<br>$\\tan\\theta=\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}$, so $\\tan\\frac\\pi4=1$.<br><br><b>Memory trick:</b> sine of 30°, 45°, 60° is $\\frac{\\sqrt1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$. Cosine is the same list backwards."],
 [2, "<b>Reading a sine graph.</b> For $y=a\\sin(b(x-h))+k$: <b>amplitude</b> $|a|$ (how far above/below the middle), <b>period</b> $\\frac{2\\pi}{b}$ (length of one wave), <b>shift</b> $h$ to the right, <b>middle line</b> $y=k$. So $y=2\\sin\\left(2\\left(x-\\frac\\pi4\\right)\\right)+1$ swings 2 either side of $y=1$ and repeats every $\\pi$.", "trigtrans", ["What is the period of $y=\\sin(3x)$?", "$\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$."]],
 [2, "<b>Solving $\\sin x=\\frac12$ on $[0,2\\pi]$ in 3 steps.</b><br><b>1.</b> Find the reference angle from the exact values: $\\frac\\pi6$.<br><b>2.</b> Sine is positive in the top half of the circle, so there are two answers: $\\frac\\pi6$ and $\\pi-\\frac\\pi6=\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$.<br><b>3.</b> For cosine (positive on the right half) use $x$ and $2\\pi-x$.<br><br><b>Never divide away a factor of $\\sin x$ or $\\cos x$:</b> you lose solutions. Factorise instead.", null, ["Solve $\\cos x=\\frac12$ for $0\\le x\\le2\\pi$.", "Reference angle $\\frac\\pi3$; cosine is positive on the right: $x=\\frac\\pi3$ and $\\frac{5\\pi}{3}$."]],
 [2, "<b>✍️ Worked problem</b> (try it on paper first). Solve $2\\sin(2x)=\\sqrt3$ for $0\\le x\\le2\\pi$.<br>Tip: widen the range first. If $0\\le x\\le2\\pi$ then $0\\le2x\\le4\\pi$ (two full turns).<br>$\\sin2x=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$, so $2x=\\frac\\pi3,\\frac{2\\pi}{3},\\frac{7\\pi}{3},\\frac{8\\pi}{3}$.<br>Divide by 2: $x=\\frac\\pi6,\\ \\frac\\pi3,\\ \\frac{7\\pi}{6},\\ \\frac{4\\pi}{3}$."],
 [3, "<b>Deeper: why $\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta=1$?</b> The point $(\\cos\\theta,\\sin\\theta)$ is on a circle of radius 1, so Pythagoras says $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1^2$.<br><br><b>Why is mains electricity a sine wave?</b> A coil spinning at constant speed has angle $\\theta=\\omega t$, so its flux $\\Phi=BA\\cos\\omega t$ and (next lesson) the emf is its rate of change, a sine wave. Every spinning generator produces circular functions."]
 ],
 qs: [
 { q: "On the unit circle, $\\sin\\theta$ is the:", o: ["y-coordinate (height)", "x-coordinate", "radius", "arc length"], a: 0, e: "The point is (cos θ, sin θ)." },
 { q: "One full turn in radians is:", o: ["2π", "π", "π/2", "360"], a: 0, e: "The circumference of the unit circle is 2π." }] },
{ id: "tc", t: "Calculus with sin, cos, tan and e", steps: [
 [1, "<b>Look at the slope of a wave.</b> Walk along $y=\\sin x$. At the start it climbs steeply (slope $+1$). At the top it is flat (slope 0). On the way down it is steepest downward ($-1$). Those slopes trace out the cosine wave. So $(\\sin x)'=\\cos x$.", "sinslope", ["What is the slope of $\\sin x$ at $x=\\frac\\pi2$?", "$\\cos\\frac\\pi2=0$: the top of the wave is flat."]],
 [1, "<b>The cycle of derivatives:</b> $\\sin\\to\\cos\\to-\\sin\\to-\\cos\\to\\sin$. Each derivative shifts the wave a quarter-turn. So $(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x$. Remember: 'cos picks up a minus on the way out'.", null, ["Differentiate $\\cos x$ twice.", "First $-\\sin x$, then $-\\cos x$."]],
 [2, "<b>With an inside (chain rule).</b> $\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin(bx)=b\\cos(bx)$ and $\\frac{d}{dx}\\cos(bx)=-b\\sin(bx)$. The $b$ comes to the front because the wave is squashed $b$ times faster, so its slopes are $b$ times steeper.<br><br>$y=3\\sin(2x)\\Rightarrow y'=6\\cos(2x)$.", null, ["Differentiate $5\\cos(3x)$.", "$-15\\sin(3x)$."]],
 [2, "<b>The exponential $e^x$.</b> It is the one function whose slope always equals its height: $(e^x)'=e^x$. With an inside, $(e^{kx})'=ke^{kx}$. Negative $k$ means decay: $(e^{-2x})'=-2e^{-2x}$ (always sloping down). This is why capacitors discharge and isotopes decay."],
 [2, "<b>$\\tan x$.</b> Since $\\tan x=\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$, the quotient rule gives $(\\tan x)'=\\frac{\\cos^2x+\\sin^2x}{\\cos^2x}=\\frac{1}{\\cos^2x}$. It is never negative (tan always rises) and blows up where $\\cos x=0$.", null, ["What is the slope of $\\tan x$ at $x=\\frac\\pi4$?", "$\\frac{1}{\\cos^2(\\pi/4)}=\\frac{1}{1/2}=2$."]],
 [2, "<b>Integrating (undoing).</b> $\\int\\cos(bx)\\,dx=\\frac1b\\sin(bx)+c$, $\\ \\int\\sin(bx)\\,dx=-\\frac1b\\cos(bx)+c$, $\\ \\int e^{kx}dx=\\frac1ke^{kx}+c$. Divide by the inside's slope, and check by differentiating.<br><br>Area under one hump of sine: $\\int_0^{\\pi}\\sin x\\,dx=[-\\cos x]_0^{\\pi}=1-(-1)=2$."],
 [2, "<b>✍️ Worked problem</b> (try it on paper first). A voltage is $v=170\\sin(100\\pi t)$ volts (50 Hz mains).<br><b>Rate of change at $t=0$:</b> $v'=170\\cdot100\\pi\\cos(100\\pi t)=17000\\pi\\approx53{,}400$ V/s.<br><b>RMS:</b> $\\frac{170}{\\sqrt2}\\approx120$ V.<br><b>Average of the positive half-cycle</b> ($0\\le t\\le0.01$): $\\frac{1}{0.01}\\int_0^{0.01}170\\sin(100\\pi t)\\,dt=\\frac{170}{0.01}\\cdot\\frac{2}{100\\pi}=\\frac{340}{\\pi}\\approx108$ V."],
 [3, "<b>Deeper: one family.</b> Euler's formula $e^{ix}=\\cos x+i\\sin x$ links them. Differentiate both sides: the left gives $ie^{ix}$ and the right gives $-\\sin x+i\\cos x=i(\\cos x+i\\sin x)$: the same thing ✓.<br><br>Also, $y''=-y$ is solved by $\\sin$ and $\\cos$. That one equation describes springs, pendulums and AC circuits (simple harmonic motion), while $y'=ky$ gives $e^{kx}$ (growth and decay)."]
 ],
 qs: [
 { q: "$\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin x=$", o: ["cos x", "-cos x", "-sin x", "sin x"], a: 0, e: "The slope of sine is cosine." },
 { q: "$\\frac{d}{dx}e^{kx}=$", o: ["ke^{kx}", "e^{kx}", "kx e^{kx-1}", "e^k"], a: 0, e: "Chain rule: e^{kx} copies, times the inside slope k." }] },
{ id: "th", t: "Thermal physics: heat, temperature and gases", steps: [
 [1, "<b>Heat is not temperature.</b> Temperature measures how fast particles jiggle on average. Heat is <i>energy moving</i> because of a temperature difference. A bath of warm water holds more heat than a cup of hot tea, even though the tea is hotter.<br><br>Kelvin: $T(\\text{K})=T(^\\circ\\text{C})+273$. Always use kelvin for gas laws.", null, ["Convert 25 °C to kelvin.", "298 K."]],
 [1, "<b>Heat moves 3 ways.</b> <b>Conduction:</b> particles bump their neighbours (a metal spoon in tea). <b>Convection:</b> fluid carries heat as it circulates (hot air rises). <b>Radiation:</b> electromagnetic waves, needing no matter (the Sun's warmth). Insulation works by trapping air: still air conducts poorly and cannot circulate."],
 [2, "<b>How much energy to warm something:</b> $Q=mc\\Delta T$. The <b>specific heat capacity</b> $c$ is the energy needed per kilogram per degree: water 4180 J/kg/K, aluminium 900, copper 385. Warm 2 kg of water by 10 K: $2\\times4180\\times10=83{,}600$ J. Water's big $c$ is why the sea warms slowly and why it makes a good coolant.", null, ["Energy to warm 0.5 kg of water by 20 K?", "$0.5\\times4180\\times20=41{,}800$ J."]],
 [2, "<b>Melting and boiling.</b> While ice melts or water boils its temperature stays <b>flat</b>: the energy goes into breaking bonds (<b>latent heat</b>), not into speeding particles up. $Q=mL$, with $L=334$ kJ/kg for melting ice and $2260$ kJ/kg for boiling water. Boiling costs about 7 times as much as melting.", "heating", ["Energy to melt 0.2 kg of ice at 0 °C?", "$0.2\\times334=66.8$ kJ."]],
 [2, "<b>Gases.</b> Gas pressure comes from particles bouncing off the walls. At constant temperature (<b>Boyle</b>): $P_1V_1=P_2V_2$, so halving the volume doubles the pressure. In general $PV=nRT$ with $T$ in kelvin and $R=8.31$ J/mol/K."],
 [2, "<b>✍️ Worked problem</b> (try it on paper first). A 0.50 kg aluminium block at 200 °C is dropped into 2.0 kg of water at 20 °C. Find the final temperature (ignore losses).<br>Heat lost = heat gained: $0.5(900)(200-T)=2(4180)(T-20)$.<br>$450(200-T)=8360(T-20)\\Rightarrow90{,}000-450T=8360T-167{,}200$.<br>$257{,}200=8810T$, so $T\\approx29.2\\,^\\circ$C."],
 [3, "<b>Deeper: why do gases obey $PV=nRT$?</b> Kinetic theory: the average kinetic energy of a particle is proportional to the kelvin temperature, $\\frac12mv^2=\\frac32kT$. Hotter means faster, so harder and more frequent wall hits.<br><br><b>Why no engine is 100% efficient:</b> heat only flows from hot to cold by itself, so some must be dumped. The best possible efficiency is $1-\\frac{T_c}{T_h}$: 600 K and 300 K gives 50%."]
 ],
 qs: [
 { q: "Heat from the Sun reaches Earth through empty space by:", o: ["radiation", "conduction", "convection", "evaporation"], a: 0, e: "Radiation needs no matter." },
 { q: "While ice melts at 0 °C its temperature:", o: ["stays constant", "rises steadily", "falls", "oscillates"], a: 0, e: "The energy goes into breaking bonds (latent heat)." }] }
);
